Chapter: Scene
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Pond

 A single frame of the Safari, computed in Codex.

 THROWAWAY, and deliberately so. This is the proof that Codex -> zig -> wasm ->
 the game's own JS blitter is a closed loop; it is not the faithful port. It
 places its own scenery instead of reading world.zig's route, and it carries its
 own Taylor sine instead of citing Gpu chapter DeviceMath, which the zig arm
 cannot transpile until real-from-int has an emitter. What it does share with the
 real thing is the part that matters: the camera, and the draw-command wire.

 The rider looks straight down a straight road, so there is no yaw rotation here
 -- geom.toRider collapses to a subtraction.

 The camera is NO LONGER hand-rolled. This chapter cites Safari chapter Camera,
 which is a faithful port of wasm/camera.zig graded against it at 1e-6 relative
 over 375 values, so the frame below is projected by the same lens the game uses
 -- FOCAL computed from the real field of view, not a number typed in here.

 We say:

Section: Draw Commands

 The wire of wasm/paint.zig: a tag, a colour, and a flat run of screen-space
 coordinates. tag 0 is a solid fill, which is all this frame uses.

  DrawCmd = record {
    tag : Integer,
    color : Integer,
    pts : List Real
  }

  project-all : List Vec3, Integer -> List Real
  project-all (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else let p = list-at ps i
      in let s = project p focal camera-w
      in [s.x, s.y] & project-all ps (i + 1)

  solid : Integer, List Vec3 -> DrawCmd
  solid (color) (ps) = DrawCmd { tag = 0, color = color, pts = project-all ps 0 }

Section: Ground

 The road is a flat quad on the ground plane, near edge first. A quad is exactly
 right for a plane: the projection is a perspective divide, so four projected
 corners bound the same region the surface does.

  road-half : Real
  road-half = 4.0

  road-near : Real
  road-near = 3.0

  road-far : Real
  road-far = 400.0

  road : DrawCmd
  road =
    (solid 3815994
    [ Vec3 { right = 0.0 - road-half, forward = road-near, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = road-half, forward = road-near, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = road-half, forward = road-far, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.0 - road-half, forward = road-far, height = 0.0 } ])

  dash : Real -> DrawCmd
  dash (a) =
    (solid 14211744
    [ Vec3 { right = 0.0 - 0.18, forward = a, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.18, forward = a, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.18, forward = a + 5.0, height = 0.0 },
      Vec3 { right = 0.0 - 0.18, forward = a + 5.0, height = 0.0 } ])

  dashes : Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  dashes (a) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then []
    else [dash a] & dashes (a + 14.0) (fuel - 1)

Section: Mountains

 A ridge far enough away to sit just above the horizon. Real geometry, not a
 screen-space cheat: the peaks are metres of height at a few kilometres of
 forward, and the projection puts them where they belong.

  ridge : Real, Integer, Real, List Real -> List Vec3
  ridge (far) (i) (x) (hs) =
    if i >= list-length hs then []
    else let h = list-at hs i
      in [Vec3 { right = x, forward = far, height = h }] & ridge far (i + 1) (x + 260.0) hs

 The x accumulator is carried as a Real rather than derived from i, because the
 zig plug has no emitter for real-from-int -- there is no way to widen a loop
 counter. Every counted loop in this port pays that, and it is the first thing
 the plug rows would buy back.

  ridge-poly : Real, Integer, List Real -> DrawCmd
  ridge-poly (far) (color) (hs) =
    let top = ridge far 0 (0.0 - 1800.0) hs
    in let base = [ Vec3 { right = 1800.0, forward = far, height = 0.0 }, Vec3 { right = 0.0 - 1800.0, forward = far, height = 0.0 } ]
    in solid color (top & base)

  mountains-far : DrawCmd
  mountains-far = ridge-poly 5200.0 7042462 [60.0, 240.0, 120.0, 300.0, 150.0, 380.0, 110.0, 260.0, 80.0, 200.0, 90.0, 170.0, 60.0, 210.0, 70.0]

  mountains-near : DrawCmd
  mountains-near = ridge-poly 3400.0 5466506 [40.0, 130.0, 200.0, 90.0, 170.0, 60.0, 220.0, 100.0, 60.0, 190.0, 80.0, 140.0, 50.0, 160.0, 40.0]

Section: Trees

 Billboards: every vertex of one tree shares a forward, so the whole tree
 projects at one depth -- the paradigm the real game uses for its scenery.

  canopy-ring : Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real -> List Vec3
  canopy-ring (right) (forward) (cy) (rad) (i) (a) =
    if i >= 9 then []
    else [Vec3 { right = right + rad * r-cos a, forward = forward, height = cy + rad * r-sin a }] & canopy-ring right forward cy rad (i + 1) (a + two-pi / 9.0)

  tree : Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  tree (right) (forward) (scale) =
    let trunk-h = 2.6 * scale
    in let trunk-w = 0.22 * scale
    in let trunk-pts = [ Vec3 { right = right - trunk-w, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }, Vec3 { right = right + trunk-w, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }, Vec3 { right = right + trunk-w, forward = forward, height = trunk-h }, Vec3 { right = right - trunk-w, forward = forward, height = trunk-h } ]
    in let leaves = canopy-ring right forward (trunk-h + 1.5 * scale) (1.9 * scale) 0 0.0
    in [solid 5915698 trunk-pts, solid 3107386 leaves]

Section: Pond

 The ported pond, placed off the left of the road. Its outline is Pond's own
 data, unchanged -- the chapter that Phase 0 graded against wasm/pond.zig, drawn
 here for the first time.

  pond-at-right : Real
  pond-at-right = 0.0 - 26.0

  pond-at-forward : Real
  pond-at-forward = 46.0

  pond-pts : List PondPt, Integer -> List Vec3
  pond-pts (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else let p = list-at ps i
      in [Vec3 { right = pond-at-right - p.cu, forward = pond-at-forward + p.cv, height = 0.0 }] & pond-pts ps (i + 1)

  pond-poly : DrawCmd
  pond-poly = solid water-color (pond-pts water-outline 0)

  bank-poly : DrawCmd
  bank-poly = solid bank-color (pond-pts bank 0)

Section: The Frame

 Paint order, back to front: the ranges, then the ground furniture, then the
 nearer trees last so they occlude.

  frame : List DrawCmd
  frame =
    let ground = [mountains-far, mountains-near, road] & (dashes 12.0 24)
    in let water = [bank-poly, pond-poly]
    in let far-trees = (tree (0.0 - 21.0) 130.0 2.0) & (tree 17.0 96.0 1.8) & (tree (0.0 - 14.0) 72.0 1.6)
    in let near-trees = (tree 11.0 44.0 1.3) & (tree (0.0 - 9.0) 30.0 1.0) & (tree 8.5 19.0 0.8)
    in ground & water & far-trees & near-trees
